%[text] %[text:anchor:T_9869F3DD] # Hellinger-Reissner Formulation for the Timoshenko Beam Boundary Value Problem
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D9C3DC9C] ## Introduction
%[text] 
%[text] This chapter discusses the application of the Hellinger-Reissner principle for the one-dimensional Timoshenko beam problem using both the Penalty and the Lagrange Multipliers methods. The objective is to obtain a transverse-shear locking-free Finite Element formulation for the Timoshenko beam problem also when using low-order basis functions. The Boundary Value Problem (BVP) associated with the Timoshenko beam problem is detailed in the [introduction](file:../2_TimoshenkoBeam/main_Chapter2_ALinearStraightTimoshenkoBeamElement.mlx:M_59CDD5EE) of [Chapter 1](file:../2_TimoshenkoBeam/main_Chapter2_ALinearStraightTimoshenkoBeamElement.mlx:H_853F8D92).
%[text] The standard weak formulation of the Timoshenko beam boundary value problem imposes strongly both subsidiary conditions regarding the constitutive equations and the kinematics in the principle of virtual work, namely,
%[text] $\\int\_0^L \\overbrace{\\delta \\left( \\frac{\\text{d} w}{\\text{d} x} + \\beta \\right) \\alpha G A \\left( \\frac{\\text{d} w}{\\text{d} x} + \\beta \\right)}^{\\text{shear part of the stiffness}} + \\overbrace{\\delta \\frac{\\text{d} \\beta}{\\text{d} x} \\, E I \\, \\frac{\\text{d} \\beta}{\\text{d} x}}^{\\text{bendingpart of the stiffness}} \\; \\text{d} x = \\overbrace{\\int\_0^L \\overline{q} \\, \\delta w + \\overline{m} \\, \\delta \\beta \\; \\text{d}x }^{\\text{source loads}} + \\overbrace{\\left\[ \\delta w \\, Q \\right\]\_0^L + \\left\[ \\delta \\beta \\, M \\right\]\_0^L}^{\\text{boundary loads}} \\;. \\quad (1)$
%[text] The Hellinger-Reissner formulation of the problem does not impose the kinematics subsidiary condition strongly, but rather in a weak sense. The corresponding variational formulation of the Timoshenko beam problem using the Hellinger-Reissner principle reads as follows:
%[text] $\\int\_0^L \\delta \\left( \\frac{\\text{d} w}{\\text{d} x} + \\beta \\right) \\, Q  \\; \\text{d} x - \\delta Q \\, \\frac{1}{\\alpha G A} \\, Q + \\delta Q \\, \\left( \\frac{\\text{d} w}{\\text{d} x} + \\beta \\right) + \\delta \\frac{\\text{d} \\beta}{\\text{d} x} \\, M - \\delta M \\, \\frac{1}{EI} \\, M + \\delta M \\, \\frac{\\text{d} \\beta}{\\text{d} x} = \\overbrace{\\int\_0^L \\overline{q} \\, \\delta w + \\overline{m} \\, \\delta \\beta \\; \\text{d}x }^{\\text{source loads}} + \\overbrace{\\left\[ \\delta w \\, Q \\right\]\_0^L + \\left\[ \\delta \\beta \\, M \\right\]\_0^L}^{\\text{boundary loads}} \\;. \\quad (2)$
%[text] where the unknown fields are the vertical deflection $w$, the cross-sectional deformation $\\beta$, the reaction transverse shear forces $Q$ and the reaction bending moments $M$, respectively. As a matter of fact the reaction transverse shear forces $Q$ and the reaction bending moments $M$act as Lagrange Multipliers for the weak imposition of the kinematics subsidiary condition.
%[text] The example setup used herein for demonstration is the same one as in Chapter 1, see [here](file:../2_TimoshenkoBeam/main_Chapter2_ALinearStraightTimoshenkoBeamElement.mlx:M_2C0E7F41). This setup is a cantilever beam subject to vertical distributed load, see the following figure accordingly:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![problemSetting_cantilever_beam.png](text:image:15d4)
%[text] As a reference solution it herein used the solution derived in script [`main_Chapter2_ALinearStraightTimoshenkoBeamElement.mlx`](file:../2_TimoshenkoBeam/main_Chapter2_ALinearStraightTimoshenkoBeamElement.mlx) and highlighted [here](file:../2_TimoshenkoBeam/main_Chapter2_ALinearStraightTimoshenkoBeamElement.mlx:H_D2431383), since both setups are equivalent.
%[text] 
%[text] [PREVIOUS Chapter](file:../5_WeakDirichletBoundaryConditions/main_Chapter5_WeakBoundaryConditions_TimoshenkoBeam.mlx:T_0DE390E8) *Weak Imposition of Dirichlet Boundary Conditions for the Timoshenko Beam Boundary Value Problem*
%[text] [NEXT Chapter](file:./main_Chapter6_HellingerReissnerFormulation_TimoshenkoBeam_Study.mlx:T_21F53CA2) *Hellinger-Reissner Formulation for the Timoshenko Beam Boundary Value Problem - A Study*
%[text] 
%[text] **Author: Andreas Apostolatos, PhD (aapostol@mathworks.com)**
%[text] **Date: 20.02.2023**
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_46B6D144] ## Preamble
clc;
clear;
close all;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_64F1BC6C] ## **Problem parameters**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DBF29B65] ### Geometric parameters
%[text] Thickness \[m\]
propStr.t = 10^(-2); %[control:slider:1064]{"position":[17,19]}
%[text] Width \[m\]
propStr.b = .1;
%[text] Height \[m^2\]
propStr.A = propStr.b*propStr.t;
%[text] X-coordinate at the left end of the beam \[m\]
X0 = 0;
%[text] Y-coordinate at the right end of the beam \[m\]
XL = 4;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1B04D3F2] ### Material paramerters
%[text] Distributed load along the beam's length \[N/m^2\]
propStr.qBar = -1e4*(propStr.t)^3;
%[text] Distributed moment along the beam's length \[N/m\]
propStr.mBar = 0;
%[text] Boundary force \[N\]
propStr.Q = 0;
%[text] Boundary moment \[Nm\]
propStr.M = 0;
%[text] Young's modulus \[N/m^2\]
propStr.E = 1e7;
%[text] Poisson ratio \[dimensionless\]
propStr.nu = 0;
%[text] Moment of inertia \[m^4\]
propStr.I = propStr.b*(propStr.t^3)/12;
%[text] Shear modulus (connected to epsilon\_12 = E/(1+nu)) \[N/m^2\]
propStr.G = propStr.E/(2*(1 + propStr.nu));
%[text] Shear correction factor \[dimensionless\]
propStr.alpha = 5/6;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0B1A74D9] ## Definition of the analytical solution
%[text] Initialization of the individual constituents
syms wEx(X) betaEx(X) QEx(X) Lsym qsym Gsym ...
    Aqsym Esym Isym alphaScf Acs
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_02B1D793] ### Analytical solution with respect to the vertical deflection
wEx(X) = (qsym*Lsym*X-qsym*X^2/2)/Gsym/(Aqsym) - ...
    (-qsym*X^4/24+qsym*Lsym*X^3/6-qsym*Lsym^2*X^2/4)/Esym/Isym;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_769B2C02] ### Derivation of the analytical solution with respect to the transverse-shear forces
%[text] Get the analytical solution of the rotational field by leveraging the Partial Differential Equation (PDE) of the problem and the functions of the [Symbolic Math Toolbox](https://www.mathworks.com/products/symbolic.html)™ in [MATLAB](https://www.mathworks.com/products/matlab.html)®
%[text] $\\beta(X) = \\int\_{0}^L - \\frac{1}{\\alpha G A}\\overline{Q} - \\frac{\\text{d}^2 w}{\\text{d} x^2} \\: \\text{d}x$
betaEx(X) = int(-1/(propStr.alpha*propStr.G*propStr.A)*propStr.qBar - ...
    diff(diff(wEx, X), X));
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_79F32117] ### Evaluation of the expressions for the given geometric and material parameters
QEx(X) = alphaScf*Gsym*Acs*(diff(wEx(X), X, 1) + betaEx(X));
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6E19AF99] ### Evaluation of the expressions for the given geometric and material parameters
wEx(X) = subs(wEx,{Lsym, qsym, Gsym, Esym, Isym, Aqsym}, ...
    {XL, propStr.qBar, propStr.G, propStr.E, ...
    propStr.b*(propStr.t^3)/12, propStr.alpha*propStr.b*propStr.t});
betaEx(X) = subs(betaEx,{Lsym, qsym, Gsym, Esym, Isym, Aqsym}, ...
    {XL, propStr.qBar, propStr.G, propStr.E, ...
    propStr.b*(propStr.t^3)/12, propStr.alpha*propStr.b*propStr.t});
QEx(X) = subs(QEx, {alphaScf, Gsym, Acs, Lsym, qsym, Gsym, Esym, ...
    Isym, Aqsym}, {propStr.alpha, propStr.G, propStr.A,XL, ...
    propStr.qBar, propStr.G, propStr.E, propStr.b*(propStr.t^3)/12, ...
    propStr.alpha*propStr.b*propStr.t});
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D184A8CA] ## Definition of the basis functions for the primal field interpolation - Vertical deflection and cross-sectional rotation field
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_26C46D06] ### Definiion of the analytical expressions for the linear basis functions of the primal field interpolation
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nN\_1 \\left(\\xi \\right)=\\frac{1-\\xi }{2},\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(3\\ldotp 1\\right)\\\\\nN\_2 \\left(\\xi \\right)=\\frac{1+\\xi }{2}\\ldotp \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(3\\ldotp 2\\right)\n\\end{array}${"editStyle":"visual"}
syms Nu1L1(xi) Nu2L1(xi) NuL1(xi)
Nu1L1(xi) = 1/2*(1 - xi);
Nu2L1(xi) = 1/2*(1 + xi);
NuL1(xi) = [Nu1L1(xi) Nu2L1(xi)];
h1 = fplot(NuL1, [-1 1]); %[output:435f7948]
xlabel("\xi"); %[output:435f7948]
ylabel("N_i^{L_1}(\xi)") %[output:435f7948]
legend("N_1^{L_1}", "N_2^{L_2}"); %[output:435f7948]
title("Linear (hat) shape functions") %[output:435f7948]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D16AA286] ### Definiion of the analytical expressions for the quadratic basis functions of the primal field interpolation
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nN\_1 \\left(\\xi \\right)=\\frac{\\xi \\;\\left(\\xi -1\\right)}{2},\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(4\\ldotp 1\\right)\\\\\nN\_2 \\left(\\xi \\right)=\\frac{\\xi \\;\\left(\\xi +1\\right)}{2}\\ldotp \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(4\\ldotp 2\\right)\\\\\nN\_3 \\left(\\xi \\right)=-\\left(\\xi -1\\right)\\;\\;\\left(\\xi +1\\right),\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(4\\ldotp 3\\right)\n\\end{array}${"editStyle":"visual"}
syms Nu1L2(xi) Nu2L2(xi) Nu3L2(xi) NuL2(xi)
Nu1L2(xi) = (xi*(xi - 1))/2;
Nu2L2(xi) = -(xi - 1)*(xi + 1);
Nu3L2(xi) = (xi*(xi + 1))/2;
NuL2(xi) = [Nu1L2(xi) Nu3L2(xi) Nu2L2(xi)];
h2 = fplot(NuL2, [-1 1]); %[output:89daa550]
xlabel("\xi"); %[output:89daa550]
ylabel("N_i^{L_2}(\xi)") %[output:89daa550]
legend("N_1^{L_2}", "N_2^{L_2}", "N_3^{L_2}"); %[output:89daa550]
title("Quadratic basis functions") %[output:89daa550]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E04C77F5] ## Definition of the basis functions for the secondary field interpolation - Lagrange Multipliers
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8DD0EE05] ## Choice of the shape functions for the discretization of the Lagrange Multipliers
%[text] ![](text:image:7a53) **Try**
%[text] Change the value of parameter choice\_shapeFunctions\_LM to obtain either a constant, a linear or a quadratic Finite Element representation of the Lagrange Multipliers field, namely, the force/moment fields
choiceShapeFunctionsLM = 0 %[control:dropdown:4f3b]{"position":[26,27]} %[output:76c2bd0f]
syms Nbeta(xi)
switch choiceShapeFunctionsLM
    case 0
%[text] Constant shape functions
%[text] $N\_1 \\left(\\xi \\right)=1,\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(8\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}
    syms Nbeta1L0(xi)
    Nbeta1L0(xi) = 1;
    Nbeta(xi) = [Nbeta1L0(xi)];
    txt = 'Constant';
%[text] 
    case 1
%[text] Linear (hat) shape functions see Eq (6)
        Nbeta(xi) = [Nu1L1(xi) Nu2L1(xi)];
        txt = 'Linear';
%[text] 
    case 2
%[text] Quadratic shape functions, see Eq (7)
        Nbeta(xi) = [Nu1L2(xi) Nu2L2(xi) Nu3L2(xi)];
        txt = 'Quadratic';
%[text] 
    otherwise
        error("Variable 'choice_shapeFunctions_LM' can only take values 0 " + ...
            "(constant shape functions), 1 (linear shape functions) " + ...
            "or 2 (quadratic shape functions) but it has instead value %d", ...
            choiceShapeFunctionsLM);
end
%[text] 
disp(txt + " basis functions are chosen for the discretization of " + ... %[output:group:00abc326] %[output:77185c80]
    "the Lagrange Multipliers fields") %[output:group:00abc326] %[output:77185c80]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9A3F17DD] ### Visualization of the shape functions chosen for the secondary field interpolation
fplot(Nbeta, [-1 1], LineWidth=2); %[output:7f66d741]
xlabel("$\xi$", Interpreter="latex"); %[output:7f66d741]
ylabel("$N_\beta$", Interpreter="latex"); %[output:7f66d741]
title("Shape functions chosen for the secondary field interpolation") %[output:7f66d741]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C04E03E6] ## Discretization
%[text] The vertical deflection and rotation fields are discretized using the shape shape functions $N\_i^u \\;\\left(\\xi \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}. Similarly, both the reaction transverse-shear forces and reaction bending moments are discretized using the same shape functions $N\_i^{\\beta } \\;\\left(\\xi \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}. Note, that the shape function sets $N\_i^u \\;\\left(\\xi \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} and $N\_i^{\\beta } \\;\\left(\\xi \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} do not have to be the same.
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nw\\left(\\xi \\right)\\approx \\sum\_{i=1:N} N\_i^u \\;w\_i ,\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(5\\ldotp 1\\right)\\\\\n\\beta \\left(\\xi \\right)\\approx \\sum\_{i=1:N} N\_i^u \\;\\beta\_i \\;\\ldotp \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(5\\ldotp 2\\right)\\\\\nQ\\left(\\xi \\right)\\approx \\sum\_{i=1:N} N\_i^{\\beta } \\;Q\_i \\;\\ldotp \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(5\\ldotp 3\\right)\\\\\nM\\left(\\xi \\right)\\approx \\sum\_{i=1:N} N\_i^{\\beta } \\;M\_i \\;\\ldotp \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(5\\ldotp 4\\right)\n\\end{array}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Here $N$ indicates the total number of nodes in the mesh. Moreover a Buvnov-Galerkin discretization is used, meaning that both the test and the unknown fields are discretized using the same basis (shape) functions.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_39E22DFB] ### Generate and display the one-dimensional mesh using linear two-noded elements
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_28FD7921] #### Mesh generation
numEl = 14; %[control:slider:8cdd]{"position":[9,11]}
mshL1 = generateLinearMeshOnLine(X0, XL, numEl);
numNodesL1 = numel(mshL1.nodes(:, 1));
isMeshNonUniform = false; %[control:checkbox:8ad7]{"position":[20,25]}
if isMeshNonUniform
    dx = mshL1.nodes(2, 1) - mshL1.nodes(1, 1);
    for ii = 2:numel(mshL1.nodes(:, 1)) - 1
        mshL1.nodes(ii, 1) = mshL1.nodes(ii, 1) + (2.*rand(1, 1) -1)/5;
    end
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3C967B2E] #### Definition of the boundary conditions
numDOFsL1 = 2*numel(mshL1.nodes(:, 1));
freeDOFsL1 = 1:numDOFsL1;
homDOFsL1 = 1:2;
[~, idxL1] = ismember(homDOFsL1, freeDOFsL1);
freeDOFsL1(idxL1) = [];
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_755ED2C9] #### Mesh plot
plotMesh1d(mshL1); %[output:412bf7f5]
xlabel("$X$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:412bf7f5]
ylabel("$Y$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:412bf7f5]
title("Finite Element discretization of the beam using " + ... %[output:group:25a4d8ee] %[output:412bf7f5]
    "two-noded linear elements") %[output:group:25a4d8ee] %[output:412bf7f5]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_40C7089A] ### Generate and display the one-dimensional mesh using quadratic three-noded elements
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_47799294] #### Mesh generation
mshL2 = generateQuadraticMeshOnLine(mshL1);
numNodes_L2 = numel(mshL2.nodes(:, 1));
isMeshNonUniform = false; %[control:checkbox:31c4]{"position":[20,25]}
if isMeshNonUniform
    for ii = numel(mshL1.nodes(:, 1)) + 1:numel(mshL2.nodes(:, 1))
        mshL2.nodes(ii, 1) = mshL2.nodes(ii, 1) + (2.*rand(1, 1) -1)/5;
    end
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C59CF499] #### Definition of the boundary conditions
numDOFsL2 = 2*numel(mshL2.nodes(:, 1));
freeDOFsL2 = 1:numDOFsL2;
homDOFsL2 = 1:2;
[~, idxL2] = ismember(homDOFsL2, freeDOFsL2);
freeDOFsL2(idxL2) = [];
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_49E4FD3C] #### Mesh plot
plotMesh1d(mshL2); %[output:0cb3bae5]
xlabel("$X$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:0cb3bae5]
ylabel("$Y$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:0cb3bae5]
title("Finite Element discretization of the beam using " + ... %[output:group:39605f03] %[output:0cb3bae5]
    "three-noded quadratic elements") %[output:group:39605f03] %[output:0cb3bae5]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E29717D9] ## **Master stiffness matrices and master consistent nodal force vectors**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A8DA32E2] ### Linear finite elements and full integration
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_74C41652] #### Computation of master stiffness matrix and load vector
computeStiffMatrixandForceVector = ...
    @computeElementStiffMatrixandForceVectorTimoshenkoBeam;
computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivs = ...
    @computeLinearBasisFunctionsAndFirstDerivatives;
dummyInp = 'undefined';
[KL1, FL1Body] = computeMasterStiffMatrixandForceVectorTimoshenkoBeam ...
    (mshL1, computeStiffMatrixandForceVector, ...
    computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivs, dummyInp, propStr);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_975BC319] #### Computation of the **nodal force vector corresponding to boundary forces**
FL1Boundary = zeros(2*numNodesL1, 1);
FL1Boundary(end - 1, 1) = propStr.Q;
FL1Boundary(end, 1) = propStr.M;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_EBD9B244] #### Sum-up of the body-force vector corresponding to distributed loads and the body-force vector corresponding to boundary loads
FL1 = FL1Body + FL1Boundary;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_391431CC] #### Visualization of the sparsity of the master stiffness matrix using linear finite elements corresponding to the full integration
spy(KL1) %[output:117cb785]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_70DBFF2D] ### **Quadratic finite elements and full integration**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_63DC627E] #### Computation of master stiffness matrix and load vector
computeStiffMatrixandForceVector = ...
    @computeElementStiffMatrixandForceVectorTimoshenkoBeam;
computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivs = ...
    @computeQuadraticBasisFunctionsAndFirstDerivatives;
dummyInp = 'undefined';
[KL2, FL2Body] = ...
    computeMasterStiffMatrixandForceVectorTimoshenkoBeam ...
    (mshL2, computeStiffMatrixandForceVector, ...
    computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivs, dummyInp, propStr);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ACF6EA98] #### Computation of the **nodal force vector corresponding to boundary forces**
FBoundaryL2 = zeros(2*numNodes_L2, 1);
FBoundaryL2(2*numNodesL1 - 1, 1) = propStr.Q;
FBoundaryL2(2*numNodesL1, 1) = propStr.M;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_AEE47E84] #### Sum-up of the body-force vector corresponding to distributed loads and the body-force vector corresponding to boundary loads
FL2 = FL2Body + FBoundaryL2;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0F941B1A] #### Visualization of the sparsity of the master stiffness matrix using linear finite elements corresponding to the full integration
spy(KL2) %[output:172b8877]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E94A6BC6] ## Hellinger-Reissner formulation of the Timoshenko beam problem
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1239F97C] ### Initialize the global Hellinger-Reissner matrices and load vector
%[text] ![](text:image:1e8d) **Try**
%[text] Change the value of variable `choice_shapeFunctions_u` to get a linear or a quadratic Finite Element representation of the unknown vertical deflection and cross-sectional rotation fields.
choiceShapeFunctionsU = 1; %[control:dropdown:03b7]{"position":[25,26]}
switch choiceShapeFunctionsU
%[text] 
    case 1
        numDOFsHR = numDOFsL1;
        NuHR = NuL1;
        mshHR = mshL1;
        freeDOFsHR = freeDOFsL1;
%[text] 
    case 2
        numDOFsHR = numDOFsL2;
        NuHR = NuL2;
        mshHR = mshL2;
        freeDOFsHR = freeDOFsL2;
%[text] 
    otherwise
        error("Variable 'choice_shapeFunctions_u' can only take values " + ...
            "1 (linear shape functions), 2 (quadratic shape functions) " + ...
            "but it has instead value %d", choiceShapeFunctionsU);
%[text] 
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F6D95100] ### 
KHR = zeros(numDOFsHR);
FHR = zeros(numDOFsHR, 1);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C31DA472] ### Looping over all the elements in the mesh
for ii = 1:numel(mshL1.elements(:, 1))
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0DDBC9E8] #### Nodal indices and nodal coordinates
    id = mshHR.elements(ii, :);
    nodesElHR = mshHR.nodes(id);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3064EBED] #### Element stiffness matrix
    [Ke, Fe] = computeElStiffMtxForceVctHellingerReissnerTimoshenkoBeam ...
        (NuHR, Nbeta, nodesElHR, propStr);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_91B3D00E] #### Element Freedom Table (EFT)
    EFT = vertcat(2*id - 1, 2*id);
    EFT = EFT(:);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A8CC081F] #### Assembly of the element contributions to the global HR matrices and load vector
    KHR(EFT, EFT) = KHR(EFT, EFT) + Ke;
    FHR(EFT) = FHR(EFT) + Fe;
%[text]  
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_90631C66] ### Sparsity pattern of the master stiffness matrix corresponding to the Hellinger-Reissner principle
spy(KHR) %[output:89460161]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9FCF820E] ## Solution of the Timoshenko beam problem
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_78261E42] ### Linear finite elements
uL1 = zeros(numDOFsL1, 1);
uL1(freeDOFsL1) = KL1(freeDOFsL1, freeDOFsL1)\FL1(freeDOFsL1);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2667D64A] ### Quadratic finite elements
uL2 = zeros(numDOFsL2, 1);
uL2(freeDOFsL2) = KL2(freeDOFsL2, freeDOFsL2)\FL2(freeDOFsL2);
[nodes_L2, idx] = sort(mshL2.nodes);
uL2Sorted = uL2(1:2:end, 1);
betaL2Sorted = uL2(2:2:end, 1);
uL2Sorted = uL2Sorted(idx);
betaL2Sorted = betaL2Sorted(idx);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_695691E2] ### Hellinger-Reissner principle
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C9DE8479] #### Solution of the linear equation system and solution postprocessing
uHR = zeros(numDOFsHR, 1);
uHR(freeDOFsHR) = KHR(freeDOFsHR, freeDOFsHR)\FHR(freeDOFsHR);
if numDOFsHR == numDOFsL1
    nodesHR = mshHR.nodes;
    wHR = uHR(1:2:end, 1);
    betaHR = uHR(2:2:end, 1);
elseif numDOFsHR == numDOFsL2
    [nodesHR, idx] = sort(mshHR.nodes);
    uHRSorted = uHR(1:2:end, 1);
    betaHRSorted = uHR(2:2:end, 1);
    wHR = uHRSorted(idx);
    betaHR = betaHRSorted(idx);
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2FF0BEE7] #### Definition of the string indicating the discretization spaces for the Hellinger-Reissner formulation
if numDOFsHR == numDOFsL1 && choiceShapeFunctionsLM == 0 %[output:group:4b1ecbde]
    strHR = "L0-L1" %[output:17a6ed37]
elseif numDOFsHR == numDOFsL1 && choiceShapeFunctionsLM == 1
    strHR = "L1-L1";
elseif numDOFsHR == numDOFsL1 && choiceShapeFunctionsLM == 2
    strHR = "L2-L1";
elseif numDOFsHR == numDOFsL2 && choiceShapeFunctionsLM == 0
    strHR = "L0-L2";
elseif numDOFsHR == numDOFsL2 && choiceShapeFunctionsLM == 1
    strHR = "L1-L2";
elseif numDOFsHR == numDOFsL2 && choiceShapeFunctionsLM == 2
    strHR = "L2-L2";
else
    error("This combination of basis functions for the primal and " + ...
        "secondary unknown fields is not supported")
end %[output:group:4b1ecbde]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FFAF1FE4] ### Postprocessing the solution
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A8FD76AB] #### Visualization of the deformed configuration
figure, %[output:3c018fdd]
plot(mshL1.nodes, uL1(1:2:end, 1), '-bo', LineWidth=2); %[output:3c018fdd]
hold on; %[output:3c018fdd]
plot(nodes_L2, uL2Sorted, '-rv', LineWidth=2); %[output:3c018fdd]
plot(nodesHR, wHR, '-ms', LineWidth=2); %[output:3c018fdd]
fplot(wEx, [X0 XL], 'k-', LineWidth=2); %[output:3c018fdd]
hold off; %[output:3c018fdd]
grid on; %[output:3c018fdd]
legend("L1", "L2", "HR " + strHR, "analytical", Location="best") %[output:3c018fdd]
xlabel("$x$", Interpreter="latex"); %[output:3c018fdd]
ylabel("$w$", Interpreter="latex"); %[output:3c018fdd]
title("Deformation") %[output:3c018fdd]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_193ACC42] #### Visualization of the cross-sectional rotation field
figure, %[output:29c9931c]
plot(mshL1.nodes, uL1(2:2:end, 1), '-bo', LineWidth=2); %[output:29c9931c]
hold on; %[output:29c9931c]
plot(nodes_L2, betaL2Sorted, '-rv', LineWidth=2); %[output:29c9931c]
plot(nodesHR, betaHR, '-m^', LineWidth=2); %[output:29c9931c]
fplot(betaEx, [X0 XL], 'k-', LineWidth=2); %[output:29c9931c]
hold off; %[output:29c9931c]
grid on; %[output:29c9931c]
legend("L1", "L2", "HR" + strHR, "analytical", Location="best") %[output:29c9931c]
xlabel("$x$", Interpreter="latex"); %[output:29c9931c]
ylabel("$w$", Interpreter="latex"); %[output:29c9931c]
title("Cross-sectional rotation $\beta$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:29c9931c]
%[text] ![](text:image:80d2) **Reflect**
%[text] How does the solution of the Hellinger-Reissner formulation compare against the analytical solution for different combinations of discretizations for the primal unknown fields (vertical deflection and cross-sectional rotation fields) and the secondary unknown fields (Lagrange Multipliers fields for the unknown reaction force and reaction moment fields)?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_45AC3756] ## Weak enforcement of the kinematics condition
%[text] In this section it is demonstrated the effect on applying the kinematics condition weakly via the Hellinger-Reissner principle. Strong enforcement of the kinematics conditions $\\gamma = \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X} + \\beta$ and $\\kappa = \\frac{\\partial \\beta}{ \\partial X}$ implies that $\\frac{1}{\\alpha GA} Q = \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X} + \\beta$ and $\\frac{1}{EI} M = \\frac{\\partial \\beta}{ \\partial X}$, respectively, since the constitutive law is strongly enforced. In this section it is demonstrated the left-hand sides of the latter equations, namely, $\\frac{1}{\\alpha GA} Q $ and $\\frac{1}{EI} M$, are not exactly equal to the right-hand sides of these equations, namely, $ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X} + \\beta$ and $\\frac{\\partial \\beta}{ \\partial X}$, respectively. The demonstration is confined only in the shear part of the stiffness, namely, the terms $\\frac{1}{\\alpha GA} Q $ and $ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X} + \\beta$.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6373CB21] ### Initialization of the auxiliary variables
syms posVctHR(xi) wHR(xi) betaHR(xi) dwdxHR(xi) gammaHR(xi) gammaHR_compl(xi)
%[text] Number of evaluation points
numPts = 50; %[control:slider:117b]{"position":[10,12]}
%[text] Evaluation points
boundingBox = [min(mshHR.nodes(:, 1)) max(mshHR.nodes(:, 1))];
XVctHR = linspace(boundingBox(1), boundingBox(2), numPts);
%[text] Initialize figure
figure, %[output:44dde734]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_54CE4DC0] ### Loop over all evaluation points
for ii = 1:numPts %[output:group:462b9731]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_81883A7D] #### **Find the element to which the evaluation point belongs** 
    if ii ~= 1
        elprev = el;
    end
    el = nan;
    for jj = 1:numel(mshHR.elements(:, 1))
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4A7C5D9A] Set up the position vector regarding the discretization of the primal fields using the Hellinger-Reissner formulation
        posVctHR(xi) = NuHR*mshHR.nodes(mshHR.elements(jj, :));
%[text] Construct the inverse position vector corresponding to the current element $\\xi(x) = x(\\xi)^{-1}$
        posVctHRInv = finverse(posVctHR);
%[text] Compute the parametric coordinate of the evaluation point in the current element using the element's inverse parametric representation
        xiParamHR = posVctHRInv(XVctHR(ii));
%[text] Check whether the computed parametric coordinate $\\xi$ is within the closed space $\[-1,1\]$. If it is, then the evaluation point belongs to the current element, otherwise not
        if xiParamHR >= -1 && xiParamHR <= 1
            el = jj;
            break;
        end
%[text] 
    end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_029A52A8] #### Verification of the element's validity containing the current point
    if isnan(el)
        error("The point with coordinate %d was found " + ...
            "in no element in the mesh", x(ii));
    end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B0FCB5D8] #### Set-up the Element Freedom Table (EFT) for the one-dimensional linear element
    EFTHR = [2*mshHR.elements(el, :) - 1; 2*mshHR.elements(el, :)];
    EFTHR = EFTHR(:);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_736D9B47] #### Setup of the expressions for the vertical deflection and rotation fields for the one-dimensional linear element
    wHR(xi) = NuHR*uHR(EFTHR(1:2:end), 1); % w(x)
    betaHR(xi) = NuHR*uHR(EFTHR(2:2:end), 1); % β(x)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6DEE68DD] #### Computation of the derivatives of the vertical deflection and rotation fields with respect to the physical space
%[text] To compute the derivatives of the basis functions with respect to the physical space given that they are defined in the parametric space, the chain rule in one-dimensiona can be used, namely:
%[text] $\\frac{\\text{d} w}{\\text{d} x} = \\frac{\\text{d} w}{\\text{d} \\xi} \\overbrace{\\frac{\\text{d} \\xi}{\\text{d} x}}^{J^{-1}}$
%[text] The inverse of the Jacobian matrix for the one-dimensional elements is given by the following expression:
%[text] $J^{-1}(\\xi) = \\frac{\\text{d} \\xi}{\\text{d} x}$
%[text] i.e. it is simply a scalar quantity. The Jacobian matrix in this case can be computed using function [`jacobian`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/sym.jacobian.html) in MATLAB:
    jacobianMtxHR = jacobian(posVctHR, xi);
%[text] Setting-up the derivatives of the vertical deflection field corresponding to the one-dimensional linear elements based on full integration with respect to the physical space
    dwdxHR(xi) = transpose(inv(jacobianMtxHR(xi)))*diff(wHR, xi);
%[text] Compute the transverse-shear strain $\\gamma(\\xi) = \\frac{\\text{d} w (\\xi)}{\\text{d} x} + \\beta(\\xi)$ at the evaluation point
    gammaHR(xi) = dwdxHR(xi) + betaHR(xi);
%[text] Primal solution $\\left( u^e,\\beta^e \\right)$ - Lagrange Multipliers $\\left( Q^e, M^e \\right)$ coupling matrix $\\mathbf{G}^e$ of the element where the evaluation point belongs to
    if ii == 1 || el ~= elprev
        [~, ~, Ge] = computeElStiffMtxForceVctHellingerReissnerTimoshenkoBeam ...
            (NuHR, Nbeta, mshHR.nodes(mshHR.elements(el, :), :), propStr);
    end
%[text] Siscrete solution of the Lagrange Multipliers $\\left( Q^e, M^e \\right)$ using the primal solution $\\left( u^e,\\beta^e \\right)$ at the evaluation point
    QhatHR = Ge(1, 1:2:end)*uHR(EFTHR(1:2:end), 1);
    MhatHR = Ge(2, 2:2:end)*uHR(EFTHR(2:2:end), 1);
%[text] Strain using the interpolation of the Lagrange Mutipliers solution
    gammaHR_compl(xi) = 1/(propStr.alpha*propStr.G*propStr.A)*Nbeta*QhatHR;
%[text] Figure of transverse-shear strain vs the kinematics condition
    fplot({gammaHR, gammaHR_compl}, [-1, 1], LineWidth=2) %[output:44dde734]
    legend("$\frac{d w (\xi)}{d x} + \beta(\xi)$", "$\frac{1}{\alpha G A} Q$", ...
        Interpreter='latex', location='best')
    mytitleText = "Difference between terms $\frac{d w (\xi)}{d x} + \beta(\xi)$ " + ...
        "and $\frac{1}{\alpha G A} Q$ at element " + num2str(el);
    title(mytitleText, Interpreter="latex")
    xlabel("$\xi$", Interpreter="latex")
    ylabel("$\frac{d w (\xi)}{d x} + \beta(\xi)$, $\frac{1}{\alpha G A} Q$", Interpreter="latex")
    drawnow;
%[text] 
end %[output:group:462b9731]
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:514f) **Try**
%[text] Plot the bending strain $\\frac{1}{EI} M$ against the change in the curvature $\\frac{\\partial \\beta}{ \\partial X}$ . You can adapt the code above in this section to enable this comparison.
%[text] ![](text:image:49b0) **Reflect**
%[text] - Is the kinematics condition in what concerns the bending part of the energy also applied weakly into the system via the Hellinger-Reissner principle?
%[text] - Is it necessary to apply the kinematics condition of the bending part of the energy weakly into the system to eliminate transverse-shear locking in the case of the Timoshenko beam problem? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_80983CE7] ## References
%[text] [\[1\] Koschnick, Frank. *Geometrische Locking-Effekte bei finiten Elementen und ein allgemeines Konzept zu ihrer Vermeidung*. Diss. München, Techn. Univ., Diss., 2004.](https://mediatum.ub.tum.de/doc/601060/0000003e.pdf)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_53AC3A57] ## **Helper functions**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_549D4A1F] ### **Element Stiffness Matrix and body force vector**
function [Ke, Fe, Ge] = ...
    computeElStiffMtxForceVctHellingerReissnerTimoshenkoBeam ...
    (Nu, Nbeta, nodesEl, propStr)
%[text] **Function description**
%[text] Returns the element stiffness matrix for an element based on the Hellinger-Reissner formulation of the 1D Timoshenko theory with four degrees of freedom per node $\\left(w,\\beta ,Q,M\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}, the vertical deflection, the rotation of the beam's cross section around the X-axis, the reaction transverse shear forces and the reaction bending moments, respectively, using full integration. The pair of unknowns for the secondary unknown fields $\\left(Q,M\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} is condensed out from the equation system. Assuming that the vector of DOFs related to the primal unknown fields $\\left(w,\\beta \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} is denoted as $\\mathbf{u}$ whereas the vector of DOFs associated with the secondary unknown fields $\\left(Q,M\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} is denoted by $\\mathbf{\\beta}$, the discrete equation system regarding the Hellinger-Reissner formulation of the Timoshenko beam problem reads as follows:
%[text] $\\left\[ \\begin{array}{cc} - \\mathbf{H} & \\mathbf{G} \\\\ \n\\mathbf{G}^{\\text{T}} & \\mathbf{0} \\end{array}\\right\] \\left\[ \\begin{array}{c} \\mathbf{\\beta} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} \\end{array} \\right\] = \\left\[ \\begin{array}{c} \\mathbf{0} \\\\ \\mathbf{F} \\end{array} \\right\]$
%[text] where,
%[text] $\\mathbf{H}^e = \\int\_{e} \\mathbf{N}\_{\\mathbf{\\beta}}^{\\text{T}} \\, \\mathbf{S} \\, \\mathbf{N}\_{\\mathbf{\\beta}} \\; \\text{d} e$,
%[text] $\\mathbf{G}^e = \\int\_{e} \\mathbf{N\n}\_{\\mathbf{\\beta}}^{\\text{T}} \\, \\mathbf{B}\_{\\mathbf{\\mathbf{u}}} \\; \\text{d} e$.
%[text] B-operator matrix $\\mathbf{B}\_{\\mathbf{\\beta}}$ is related to the discretization of the pair $\\left(Q,M\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} and it is given by the following equation,
%[text] $\\mathbf{N}\_{\\mathbf{\\beta}} = \\left\[ \\begin{array}{ccccc} N\_1^{\\mathbf{\\beta}} & 0 & \\ldots & N\_{n\_{\\mathbf{\\beta}}}^{\\mathbf{\\beta}} & 0 \\\\ \n0 & N\_1^{\\mathbf{\\beta}} & \\ldots & 0 & N\_{n\_{\\mathbf{\\beta}}}^{\\mathbf{\\beta}}\\end{array} \\right\]$,
%[text] since derivatives on the secondary pair of unknown fields do not appear, also piecewise constant functions can be used as basis functions. B-operator matrix $\\mathbf{B}\_{\\mathbf{u}}$ is related to the discretization of the shear deformation $\\gamma = \\frac{\\text{d} w}{\\text{d} x} + \\beta$ and it has been introduced already in the standard variational formulation of the Timoshenko beam. Finally, the compliance matrix $\\mathbf{S}$ is given by the following relationship,
%[text] $\\mathbf{S} = \\left\[ \\begin{array}{cc} \\frac{1}{\\alpha G A} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{EI} \\end{array} \\right\]$, which is basically the inverse of the material matrix in this case.
%[text]  **Input :**
%[text]  `Nu` : Vector containing the symbolic expression for the basis functions regarding the pair of unknown fields $\\left( w, \\beta\\right)$
%[text]  `Nbeta` : Vector containing the symbolic expression for the basis functions regarding the pair of unknown fields $\\left( Q, M \\right)$
%[text]  `nodesEl` : Nodal coordinates of the finite element arranged in a column-vector
%[text]  `propStr` : Structure containing the following fields,
%[text]  `.A` : Cross sectional area of the beam
%[text]  `.q` : Distributed load on the beam
%[text]  `.m` : Distributed moment on the beam
%[text]  `.E` : Young's modulus
%[text]  `.nu` : Poisson's ratio
%[text]  `.I` : Moment of inertia
%[text]  `.G` : Shear modulus
%[text]  `.alpha` : Shear correction factor
%[text]  **Output :**
%[text]  `Ke` : Element stiffness matrix of a Timoshenko beam
%[text]  `Fe` : Element force vector of the Timoshenko beam
%[text]  `Ge` : Element coupling matrix $\\mathbf{G}^e = \\int\_{L^e} \\mathbf{N}\_\\beta \\mathbf{B}\_u \\: \\text{d}L^e$
%[text] **Function implementation**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F7B30448] #### Read-input
if numel(nodesEl) ~= numel(Nu(0))
    error("The number of nodes %d do not match the number " + ...
        "of basis functions %d", numel(nodesEl), numel(Nu(0)))
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_497D88C6] #### Set-up the geometric map $x(\\xi) = \\sum\_{i=1}^n N\_i(\\xi) x\_i$:
syms xi
numNodesuEl = numel(Nu(xi));
numNodesbetaEl = numel(Nbeta(xi));
xel = sym('xel', [numNodesuEl 1]);
x = Nu*xel;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4B5D134A] #### Get the polynomial order of the element
pEl = numNodesuEl - 1;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9063602A] #### Get the number of DOFs per element
numDOFsuEl = 2*numNodesuEl;
numDOFsbetaEl = 2*numNodesbetaEl;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DAC77F83] #### Set-up the Jacobian $J(\\xi) = \\frac{\\text{d} x(\\xi)}{\\text{d} \\xi} = \\sum\_{i = 1}^n \\frac{\\text{d} N\_i(\\xi)}{\\text{d} \\xi}x\_i$:
J = jacobian(x, xi);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A3CFE2FA] #### Transform the derivatives of the basis functions from the parametric to the physical space
%[text] $\\frac{\\text{d} N\_i (\\xi)}{\\text{d} x} = \\frac{\\text{d} N\_i (\\xi)}{\\text{d} \\xi} \\frac{\\text{d} \\xi}{\\text{d} x} = \\frac{\\text{d} N\_i (\\xi)}{\\text{d} \\xi} J^{-1}$ 
syms dN(xi)
dN(xi) = J\diff(Nu, xi);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_32E13393] #### Computation of the material matrix
syms alphaScf G A E I
S = [1/(alphaScf*G*A) 0
     0                 1/(E*I)];
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6BAC9BCE] #### Symbolic formulation of the basis function matrix
Nmtx = sym(zeros(2, numDOFsuEl));
for ii = 1:numNodesuEl
    filtermtx = zeros(numNodesuEl, 1);
    filtermtx(ii) = 1;
    Nmtx(1, 2*ii - 1) = Nu*filtermtx;
    Nmtx(2, 2*ii) = Nu*filtermtx;
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E0F16DF5] #### Set-up the B-Operator matrix for the pair vertical deflections - cross sectional rotations symbolically
Bumtx = sym(zeros(2, numDOFsuEl));
for ii = 1:numNodesuEl
    filtermtx = zeros(numNodesuEl, 1);
    filtermtx(ii) = 1;
    Bumtx(1, 2*ii - 1) = dN*filtermtx;
    Bumtx(1, 2*ii) = Nu*filtermtx;
    Bumtx(2, 2*ii) = dN*filtermtx;
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1B13891A] #### Formulation of the B-operator matrix for the pair shear forces - bending moments
Nbetamtx = sym(zeros(2, numDOFsbetaEl));
for ii = 1:numNodesbetaEl
    filtermtx = zeros(numNodesbetaEl, 1);
    filtermtx(ii) = 1;
    Nbetamtx(1, 2*ii - 1) = Nbeta*filtermtx;
    Nbetamtx(2, 2*ii) = Nbeta*filtermtx;
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DE60F222] #### Set up element stiffness matrix and load vector integrands
syms q m HeIntegrand(xi) GeIntegrand(xi) FeIntegrand(xi)
HeIntegrand(xi) = transpose(Nbetamtx)*S*Nbetamtx;
GeIntegrand(xi) = transpose(Nbetamtx)*Bumtx;
FeIntegrand(xi) = transpose(Nmtx)*[q; m];
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_145AC673] #### Choose method for computing the element stiffness matrix
integrationMethod = 2; %[control:dropdown:3091]{"position":[21,22]}
if integrationMethod == - 1
    if numNodesuEl == 2
        method = 1;
    elseif numNodesuEl == 3
        method = 2;
    else
        method = 2;
    end
else
    method = integrationMethod;
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9A5EEF70] #### Compute the symbolic expression of the element stiffness matrix and load vector based on the selected method
switch method
%[text] *1.) Method 1: Perform the variable substitution* $\\xi =\\xi \\left(X\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} which is the inverse of map $X=X\\left(\\xi \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}:
    case 0
%[text] $B\\left(\\xi \\right)=B\\left(\\xi \\left(X\\right)\\right)=\\bar{B} \\left(X\\right),${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $\\int\_{X\_1 }^{X\_2 } B^T \\left(\\xi \\right)\\textrm{CB}\\left(\\xi \\right)\\;\\textrm{dx}=\\int\_{X\_1 }^{X\_2 } {\\bar{B} }^T \\left(x\\right)C\\bar{B} \\left(x\\right)\\;\\textrm{dx}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $\\int\_{X\_1 }^{X\_2 } N^T \\left(\\xi \\right)p\\left(\\xi \\right)\\;\\textrm{dx}=\\int\_{X\_1 }^{X\_2 } {\\bar{N} }^T \\left(x\\right)\\bar{p} \\left(x\\right)\\;\\textrm{dx}${"editStyle":"visual"}
        syms X
        Xxi = solve(X == x(xi), xi);
        HeIntBar = subs(HeIntegrand, xi, Xxi);
        GeIntBar = subs(GeIntegrand, xi, Xxi);
        FeIntBar = subs(FeIntegrand, xi, Xxi);
        HeInt = int(HeIntBar, X, nodesEl(1), nodesEl(2));
        GeInt = int(GeIntBar, X, nodesEl(1), nodesEl(2));
        FeInt = int(FeIntBar, X, nodesEl(1), nodesEl(2));
%[text] *2.) Method 2: Transform the element stiffness matrix in the element's parent space \[-1 , 1\]:*
    case 1
%[text] $\\int\_{X\_1 }^{X\_2 } B^T \\left(\\xi \\right)\\textrm{CB}\\left(\\xi \\right)\\;\\textrm{dX}=\\int\_{-1}^{+1} B^T \\left(\\xi \\right)\\textrm{CB}\\left(\\xi \\right)\\;\\det \\left(J\\left(\\xi \\right)\\right)\\;d\\xi${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $\\int\_{X\_1 }^{X\_2 } N\\left(\\xi \\right)p\\left(\\xi \\right)\\;\\textrm{dX}=\\int\_{-1}^{+1} N\\left(\\xi \\right)p\\left(\\xi \\right)\\;\\det \\left(J\\left(\\xi \\right)\\right)\\;d\\xi${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Symbolic computation of the element stiffness matrix
        HeInt = int(HeIntegrand*det(J), xi, -1, 1);
        GeInt = int(GeIntegrand*det(J), xi, -1, 1);
        FeInt = int(FeIntegrand*det(J), xi, -1, 1);
%[text] *3.) Method 3: Transform the element stiffness matrix in the element's parent space \[-1 , 1\] and perform Gaussian integration*
    case 2
%[text] $\\int\_{X\_1 }^{X\_2 } B^T \\left(\\xi \\right)\\textrm{CB}\\left(\\xi \\right)\\;\\textrm{dx}=\\int\_{-1}^{+1} B^T \\left(\\xi \\right)\\textrm{CB}\\left(\\xi \\right)\\;\\det \\left(J\\left(\\xi \\right)\\right)\\;d\\xi =\\sum\_{i=1}^2 B^T \\left(\\xi\_i \\right)\\textrm{CB}\\left(\\xi\_i \\right)\\;\\det \\left(J\\left(\\xi\_i \\right)\\right)w\_i${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $\\int\_{X\_1 }^{X\_2 } N\\left(\\xi \\right)p\\left(\\xi \\right)\\;\\textrm{dx}=\\int\_{-1}^{+1} N\\left(\\xi \\right)p\\left(\\xi \\right)\\;\\det \\left(J\\left(\\xi \\right)\\right)\\;d\\xi =\\sum\_{i=1}^2 N\\left(\\xi\_i \\right)p\\left(\\xi\_i \\right)\\;\\det \\left(J\\left(\\xi\_i \\right)\\right)w\_i${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Symbolic computation of the element stiffness matrix
        numGP = pEl + 1;
        [xiGP, GW_xi] = getGaussPointsAndWeightsOverUnitDomain(numGP);
        HeInt = sym(zeros(numDOFsbetaEl));
        GeInt = sym(zeros(numDOFsbetaEl, numDOFsuEl));
        FeInt = sym(zeros(numDOFsuEl, 1));
        for ii = 1:height(GW_xi)
            HeInt = HeInt + HeIntegrand(xiGP(ii))*det(J(xiGP(ii)))*GW_xi(ii);
            GeInt = GeInt + GeIntegrand(xiGP(ii))*det(J(xiGP(ii)))*GW_xi(ii);
            FeInt = FeInt + FeIntegrand(xiGP(ii))*det(J(xiGP(ii)))*GW_xi(ii);
        end
%[text] Assert an error in case neither of cases 0, 1, 2 or 3 is chosen:
    otherwise
        error("Variable 'method' should take the values 0, 1, 2 or 3 " + ...
            "but it has instead the value %d\n", method);
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_51E5D2E8] #### Numerical evaluation of the element stiffness matrix and load vector
He = subs(HeInt, [alphaScf, G, E, A, I, transpose(xel)], ...
    [propStr.alpha, propStr.G, propStr.E, propStr.A, ...
    propStr.I, transpose(nodesEl)]);
Ge = subs(GeInt, transpose(xel), transpose(nodesEl));
Fe = subs(FeInt, [q, m, transpose(xel)], ...
    [propStr.qBar, propStr.mBar, transpose(nodesEl)]);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A12A1303] #### Compute the part of the stiffness matrix associated purely with the vertical deflection - cross sectional rotation DOFs
Ke = transpose(Ge)*(He\Ge); % Simplification for the expression transpose(Ge)*inv(He)*Ge
%[text] 
end

